# Fixed 2026-11-27, defect 2871, lane sig-family-2 (panel 262's
# ffi-pragmatist, `gtc`): the handler of the first refused case run on an
# alternate signal stack the program gives (`SA_ONSTACK`), so the frame
# records the runtime reads are those of the interrupted stack, not the
# handler's.
# Symptom: Bus error, exit 138, once, and a false `panic: stack exhausted`,
# exit 134, twice, in 3 runs on this Mac; Segmentation fault, exit 139, 3 of 3
# in Linux arm64.
# Cause: a switch loads the target's signal mask before its registers, so a
# signal pending while blocked runs its handler inside the switch, on the
# stack being left; a handler that then saves into or loads a context the
# interrupted switch has yet to finish makes it resume frames that have
# returned, a call POSIX leaves undefined, neither `swapcontext` nor
# `setcontext` being async-signal-safe.
# Fix: each wrapper of `swapcontext` and `setcontext` notes the switch it
# begins (`runtime/parts/sigguard.c`), a delivery whose frame records reach
# that wrapper's frame keeps the switch's two contexts, and a switch its
# handler makes naming either stops the program before it switches, saying
# why; a handler's switch naming other contexts runs.
extern "fixedbugs-2871-a-handler-switching-contexts-inside-a-switch.h"
    function sw_run(shape: i32, cb: (function(i64) -> i64)) -> i64

function answer(n: i64) -> i64
    print("answer ran for ", n)
    return n * 10

function main()
    print("sw_6: ", sw_run(shape: 6, cb: answer))
